"""Original applications for paper bots, C research engines, and local Web3 projects."""

APPLICATIONS = [
    ("5.6", "knight", "A bot specification must define the authority and limits of a program before it can submit orders.", "Write a fictional paper-bot contract naming its instruments, signal frequency, maximum gross exposure, allowed order types, stale-data rule, and the events that disable new orders.", "Use measurable limits and observable event conditions, then map each to a check and an owner. A profit target is not a control specification. Include how the account will be reconciled and how a halted system becomes eligible to resume."),
    ("5.1", "idempotency", "An event-driven architecture needs stable identities so delayed messages do not become new trading intentions.", "Draw a fictional event path from signal to order intent, broker acceptance, partial fill, and portfolio update. Assign identifiers and distinguish a new intent from a retry of an existing one.", "Preserve the intent ID across transport retries and give execution events their own deduplication keys. Replaying the same fill must leave the same economic state. Components should exchange explicit events rather than infer a fill from successful message delivery."),
    ("5.7", "negative_prices", "Market-data validation must know which product and clock a field belongs to.", "A fictional feed mixes cash-equity quotes and energy-futures quotes. Define product-specific price rules and event-time/receipt-time fields, then test zero, negative, stale, and out-of-order values.", "Reject nonpositive cash-equity prices under the course contract, while applying the supported futures product definition separately. Preserve both timestamps and flag ordering violations. One universal positive-price check would reject valid values for some instruments or conceal an incorrectly classified feed."),
    ("5.8", "ml_pipeline", "A reproducible feature must use exactly the observations available before the bot's decision.", "A fictional bot computes a five-close mean after each close. Specify its warm-up rule, missing-session behavior, and the earliest execution time for the resulting signal.", "Require five valid eligible closes or use a declared alternative; never silently shorten the window. Timestamp the feature after its last input is available and execute later. Store feature version and input identity so replay can explain a changed signal."),
    ("5.2", "warung", "A simple business rule is useful only when its conditions and resulting actions are unambiguous.", "Translate a fictional value screen into a paper-bot rule: buy eligible companies with positive operating cash flow and a cash yield above a stated threshold, subject to a position cap.", "Define source availability, denominator validity, ranking ties, and what happens when no company qualifies. Separate selection from sizing and order submission. A readable verbal rule still needs deterministic behavior at missing values, threshold equality, and portfolio constraints."),
    ("5.9", "bi_rate", "A trend bot responds to observed market changes while paying for repeated entries and exits.", "Use an invented price path around a rate announcement to compare a fast breakout rule with a slower trend rule. Record next-observation fills, reversals, and costs for both.", "Keep the announcement as dated context and prevent either rule from using future prices. Count turnover as well as gross returns. A fast rule may respond sooner but whipsaw more often; one favorable shock is insufficient evidence for choosing its parameters."),
    ("5.10", "forecasting", "Mean reversion is a hypothesis about a defined deviation, not a reason to buy every falling price.", "A fictional pair spread widens from its historical mean after one company loses a major customer. State the entry rule and a separate test for a changed business relationship.", "A statistical threshold can fire while the original equilibrium is no longer credible. Include a break or invalidation policy and limit unhedged exposure. Re-estimating on the full future path would hide how the bot actually encountered and handled the failure."),
    ("5.11", "bca_split", "A bot backtest must handle dated transformations without inventing returns or same-bar execution.", "Create a fictional five-for-one split fixture with a close-based signal. Specify share-count changes, adjusted feature history, fees, and the next eligible execution event.", "The split alone preserves position value. Keep the feature adjustment convention consistent with the accounting convention and avoid double adjustment. A signal formed from the closing price cannot receive an earlier fill at that same close unless a separately justified execution model permits it."),
    ("5.12", "flash_crash", "Execution quality depends on the price available when an order arrives, not just the last printed trade.", "A fictional buy decision uses midpoint 100. Ten shares fill at 101 and ten at 102, with total fees of 2. Calculate the volume-weighted fill and implementation shortfall against the decision midpoint.", "The average fill is 101.5. Price shortfall is 30 and fees add 2, for total shortfall 32 on a 2,000 reference notional, or 1.6%. State the benchmark time and include unfilled quantity separately when relevant."),
    ("5.13", "idempotency", "Cancellation is a request that can race with execution rather than instantly erase an order.", "A fictional 100-share order fills 30, receives a cancel request, then fills another 20 before cancellation confirmation. Reconcile final filled, canceled, and position quantities.", "Fifty shares executed and the remaining 50 were canceled. The position follows execution records, not the requested cancellation time. Deduplicate both fill and status messages, and permit valid late executions according to the broker's event semantics."),
    ("5.14", "flash_crash", "An execution schedule must respect available volume as market conditions change.", "A fictional paper order needs 12,000 shares across three intervals with expected volumes of 20,000, 30,000, and 10,000. Compare equal slices with a 10% participation limit.", "TWAP proposes 4,000 per interval, while the participation caps are 2,000, 3,000, and 1,000. Only 6,000 fit the total cap. A scheduler must defer or reduce the order instead of claiming completion at an impossible participation rate."),
    ("5.5", "atomic_ledger", "A paper broker needs a cash-and-position journal that explains every balance change.", "A fictional account starts with 1,000 cash, buys four shares at 100 with a fee of 1, then sells one at 110 with another fee of 1. Reconcile cash and remaining shares.", "Cash becomes 599 after the buy and 708 after the sale; three shares remain. At a mark of 110, equity is 1,038. Persist execution, fee, cash, and position updates as one consistent transition and replay them to verify the balance."),
    ("5.4", "knight", "Sizing rules need hard limits that remain effective when a signal or deployment behaves unexpectedly.", "A fictional bot requests 300 shares at 100 while current equity is 50,000 and the single-position cap is 20%. Determine the largest new position if no shares or pending orders already exist.", "The notional cap is 10,000, allowing at most 100 shares before fees and other reserves. Reject or clip using a documented policy. Repeat with existing and pending exposure; checking only the new order misses commitments already consuming the limit."),
    ("5.15", "idempotency", "Outstanding orders consume buying capacity before they appear in filled positions.", "A fictional account has 10,000 cash and an accepted buy reserving 6,000. A second strategy requests 5,000. Calculate available uncommitted cash and decide whether both requests fit.", "Only 4,000 remains before fees and safety buffers, so the second request does not fit unchanged. Use one account-level reservation policy across strategies. A timeout must not release the first reservation until order state is reconciled or cancellation is confirmed."),
    ("5.16", "knight", "A pre-trade gate makes safety conditions independent of the component proposing a trade.", "A fictional signal service proposes an order with a stale quote, an unknown symbol, and notional above the account limit. Define deterministic rejection reasons and the evidence retained for review.", "Validate instrument identity, data freshness, quantity/price units, buying capacity, and limits before submission. Rejection should preserve the original intent and rule version without creating a broker side effect. Test each condition independently and combinations that could expose ordering mistakes."),
    ("5.17", "knight", "A halt needs a controlled recovery process that starts from reconciled account state.", "A fictional paper bot breaches a daily loss limit while two orders remain live. Specify what stops immediately, how live orders are handled, and the evidence required before resumption.", "Disable new risk, follow the declared cancellation or risk-reduction policy, and continue consuming execution events. Reconcile positions, cash, and remaining commitments with the broker. Resumption requires an identified cause and passed checks, not merely restarting the process or resetting the loss counter."),
    ("5.18", "ml_pipeline", "Overlapping trading outcomes can cross a time split even when feature dates do not.", "A fictional training trade starts Friday and exits Wednesday, while validation begins Monday. Mark its label interval and decide whether it belongs in the training fold.", "The label includes validation-period outcomes, so purge it from that training boundary. Use timestamps for actual event horizons rather than a guessed number of rows. Group related same-date observations and keep any model selection inside the permitted earlier data."),
    ("5.19", "calibration", "The mapping from a forecast to a position should be explicit enough to evaluate separately from the model.", "Compare a fictional regression forecast with a zero-position baseline and a fixed-rule baseline. Define a dead zone around zero and a capped position mapping before measuring returns.", "The mapping determines exposure, turnover, and response to small noisy predictions. Fix it using training/validation data and apply identical execution assumptions to baselines. A lower prediction error can still produce worse net trading outcomes through excessive churn or concentrated positions."),
    ("5.20", "ml_pipeline", "Boosted trees can exploit data artifacts unless constraints and validation reflect the trading problem.", "A fictional stock model uses liquidity and valuation features. Propose a depth limit and an ablation for a suspicious identifier feature, then explain when a monotonic constraint needs economic justification.", "Compare on chronological validation with preprocessing fitted inside each fold. An identifier can encode dataset selection rather than transferable information. Constraints are modeling assumptions; impose them only where the feature definition and expected direction support them, and inspect errors where the constraint is inappropriate."),
    ("5.21", "calibration", "A probability threshold stops being reliable when reported confidence and realized frequency diverge.", "For 200 fictional predictions near 0.7, only 100 mature outcomes are successes. Describe the calibration finding and a response that preserves an independent future evaluation period.", "Observed frequency is 0.5 in that bucket, indicating overconfidence subject to sampling uncertainty. Inspect regime and label quality, then fit any recalibration on eligible prior data. Do not retune thresholds on the same future outcomes used to claim improvement."),
    ("5.3", "llm_evidence", "An AI research layer should produce evidence-backed proposals within a clearly defined authority boundary.", "A fictional assistant reads filings and recommends changing a paper portfolio. Specify which outputs it may generate and which independent component must validate quantities, sources, and trading constraints.", "Allow structured research claims and proposed actions with source passages, while deterministic checks verify numbers and account limits. Retrieved text cannot grant new tool permissions. Keep the final action path explicit so a fluent explanation cannot bypass the bot's established controls."),
    ("5.22", "edgar", "Retrieval must preserve the filing version that supports a claim at a specific decision time.", "Design an evidence record for a fictional revenue claim with accession ID, filing date, fiscal period, units, source passage, and extraction timestamp. Handle a later amendment without replacing the old record.", "Store the amendment as a new version linked to the original. A reviewer can then reproduce the earlier claim and see why it changed. Document identity and availability belong beside the number; a bare copied value loses both provenance and chronological meaning."),
    ("5.23", "warung", "Structured extraction can still be numerically wrong when units and accounting relationships are ignored.", "A fictional report states revenue 120 million and expenses 90 million, but an extracted field labels revenue as 120 billion. Define schema and reconciliation checks that should reject the output.", "Require currency and scale separately, normalize units, and compare reported totals with accounting relationships and the cited passage. Profit under the intended simplified values is 30 million. Schema-valid JSON alone does not establish that a financial claim is supported or correctly scaled."),
    ("5.24", "llm_evidence", "Documents can contain adversarial instructions that try to cross from evidence into tool control.", "Insert a harmless test sentence into a fictional filing asking the assistant to ignore limits and call an order tool. Specify the expected response and an audit assertion.", "The assistant should treat the sentence as untrusted document content and continue the authorized extraction task. Tool permissions and deterministic limits must remain unchanged. Test actual absence of the prohibited tool effect, not just whether the assistant says it ignored the instruction."),
    ("5.25", "overload", "Retries and unbounded queues can amplify a slow service into a stale-data incident.", "A fictional consumer processes 80 events per second while 120 arrive for 30 seconds. Calculate backlog growth, then define a queue limit and a retry budget.", "The backlog grows by 1,200 events if all arrivals are queued. Bound queue size and age, distinguish retryable failures, and introduce admission control or safe coalescing where semantics permit. Never discard executions as though they were replaceable quote updates."),
    ("5.26", "atomic_ledger", "Crash recovery must restore a complete economic transition rather than half of a fill.", "Inject a fictional crash after a fill record is written but before its cash update. Explain how a transactional journal and replay checkpoint should avoid an inconsistent recovered account.", "Commit the fill and dependent balances atomically, or derive balances from an authoritative durable event log with idempotent replay. Recovery must produce either the prior state or the complete next state. Test interruption at each persistence boundary, including a repeated delivery after restart."),
    ("5.27", "knight", "Useful monitoring connects technical symptoms to the portfolio consequences that require action.", "Design a fictional incident dashboard showing order rate, rejected intents, oldest quote age, live commitments, position drift, and release version. Assign an action to each critical alert.", "An alert should identify the affected account or instrument and the next diagnostic or control step. Preserve correlated event IDs and timestamps. A dashboard full of CPU graphs may miss runaway order creation or disagreement between expected and actual positions."),
    ("5.28", "model_release", "A release gate needs evidence that the new package reproduces behavior and can be reversed.", "Prepare a fictional paper release checklist with a signed-off manifest, fixture results, shadow comparison, environment compatibility, state migration plan, and rollback trigger.", "Run the candidate against fixed inputs and compare decisions and account transitions before activation. Rollback must address durable state as well as code; an older binary may not understand a new schema. Keep the previous approved artifact and a rehearsed recovery path."),
    ("5.33", "bca_split", "A live paper account and its research features must agree on a corporate action's effective session.", "A fictional five-for-one split occurs between sessions. Define transformations for held shares, historical indicators, pending-order prices/quantities under your mock broker contract, and symbol metadata.", "Document each transformation and whether the mock broker cancels or adjusts pending orders. Preserve economic value under the mechanical change and test replay across the boundary. Do not assume every real venue handles orders identically; the simulator's contract must be explicit."),
    ("5.34", "flash_crash", "Order-book replay must respect queue position and available depth rather than fill every order at the best quote.", "A fictional bid queue at 100 contains 300 shares ahead of your 100-share order. An incoming market sell consumes 350 shares. Calculate your fill under strict price-time priority.", "The first 300 shares fill earlier orders and 50 fill yours, leaving 50 outstanding. This simplified model assumes no hidden liquidity or intervening events. Replay cancellations and arrivals in sequence, and disclose where the data cannot establish true queue position."),
    ("5.35", "idempotency", "Multiple target updates can overspend one account unless orders reconcile against a shared commitment ledger.", "A fictional portfolio targets 6,000 in A and 5,000 in B with 10,000 cash. An A order already reserves 4,000. Compute the remaining total requirement before choosing a cash-budget policy.", "The desired total is 11,000, exceeding cash by 1,000 before costs. Count the existing 4,000 reservation toward A's target rather than submitting another 6,000. Scale, prioritize, or reject according to a deterministic account-level rule and reconcile subsequent fills."),
    ("5.36", "flash_crash", "Execution evaluation must retain the quantity that failed to trade, not just favorable completed fills.", "A fictional buy target is 100 shares at decision price 100. Only 60 fill at 101; the evaluation mark is 103. Compute filled price shortfall and unfilled opportunity cost, excluding fees.", "Filled shortfall is 60×1 = 60 and unfilled opportunity cost is 40×3 = 120, totaling 180 under this convention. State the endpoint and side. Reporting only the average fill would hide most of the implementation cost in this example."),
    ("5.37", "factors", "Ranked portfolios need deterministic tie handling and an explicit route from scores to affordable orders.", "A fictional momentum bot has three equally ranked stocks competing for two slots. Specify a stable tie-breaker, historical eligibility rules, weight caps, and handling of an unaffordable residual order.", "Use a declared tie-breaker independent of future returns, then apply account-level constraints and lot rounding. Record cash left by rounding and failed eligibility checks. Reproducibility includes these edge decisions; a ranking formula alone cannot determine the actual portfolio."),
    ("5.38", "forecasting", "A pairs bot's hedge ratio and break controls must be estimated without using the episode being evaluated.", "A fictional pair's training hedge ratio is 1.5, then a later structural change shifts the relation. Specify the frozen evaluation, residual monitoring, and a dated rule for retraining or stopping.", "Size the original trade using the training estimate and record the deterioration rather than retroactively replacing the ratio. A monitoring threshold should be set before the break. Retraining creates a new model version and still needs evidence that the economic relationship remains meaningful."),
    ("5.39", "ldi", "Volatility targets can create concentrated liquidity demands when several sleeves deleverage together.", "Two fictional sleeves both halve exposure after a common volatility shock. Map their overlapping holdings and estimate aggregate shares to sell before checking each sleeve's individual risk target.", "Net or combine intended trades at the portfolio level, then compare aggregate demand with available liquidity and cash requirements. Individually sensible adjustments can crowd the same exit. Report residual risk if execution limits prevent reaching the target immediately."),
    ("5.40", "flash_crash", "A market maker's quoted spread must compensate for inventory risk and adverse selection as well as fees.", "A fictional market maker buys ten units at bid 99, then the midpoint falls from 100 to 97 before it can sell. Calculate marked inventory P&L and explain why the quoted spread was not locked-in profit.", "The inventory is marked down by 20 relative to its purchase cost. Earning a spread requires subsequent execution, which may occur after information changes the price. Track inventory limits, quote freshness, and conditional post-fill price moves alongside gross spread capture."),
    ("5.41", "ml_pipeline", "Nested evaluation needs interval-aware purging at both the inner and outer boundaries.", "Sketch fictional outer monthly test windows and inner tuning windows for trades lasting up to ten sessions. Show how actual trade-end times determine purging in both loops.", "Remove training labels whose intervals cross the relevant future boundary, then fit preprocessing and model selection within the remaining history. A fixed date split alone is insufficient. Save each outer prediction before labels mature and never tune on the assembled outer results."),
    ("5.42", "reporting", "A trial ledger and dependence-aware uncertainty reveal how much evidence supports a selected bot.", "A fictional team runs 25 related rules on the same market history. Design a report linking all trials and a block-bootstrap comparison with a simple baseline.", "Preserve the shared date structure when resampling paired strategy and baseline returns. Disclose selection, block-length sensitivity, and unsuccessful variants. Treat the reported winner as the output of a search process rather than a rule that was uniquely specified before the sample."),
    ("5.43", "ldi", "Borrow and sale capacity can disappear together when a supposedly hedged strategy needs them most.", "A fictional long-short bot faces doubled spreads, halved tradable volume, and a borrow recall on the short leg. Specify a combined stress with timed cover requirements and limited execution capacity.", "Reconcile what can actually trade by each deadline and show the resulting cash, gross exposure, and unhedged risk. Applying each stress separately may miss their interaction. A backtest must not assume unlimited borrow or costless instantaneous liquidation to preserve neutrality."),
    ("5.44", "atomic_ledger", "Independent engines should agree on economic transitions before their summary returns are trusted.", "Build a fictional fixture with one buy, a partial sell, two fees, and a split. Compare vectorized research and event-driven replay at every timestamp, naming tolerances for any floating-point fields.", "Match cash, shares, fees, and marked equity after each event, not merely the final Sharpe ratio. A discrepancy should resolve to a specific event or convention. Keep integer quantities and exact monetary units where appropriate, with explicit rounding policies for the rest."),
    ("5.45", "calibration", "A meta-label is meaningful only relative to the base strategy's actual entry and exit rules.", "A fictional base rule proposes a trade with gain 4, loss 2, and cost 0.4. Find the success-probability threshold for accepting the trade and define the label's outcome interval.", "Expected payoff is 6p − 2.4, so break-even is 0.4 under these assumptions. Labels must follow the stated fills, exits, and costs, with overlapping intervals handled in validation. A classifier trained on different hypothetical trades cannot directly justify this acceptance threshold."),
    ("5.46", "llm_evidence", "Citation quality requires checking whether the passage supports the claim, not merely whether a link exists.", "Create a fictional evaluation packet with one supported revenue claim, one wrong-unit claim, and one question the document cannot answer. Define expected extraction, correction, and abstention outcomes.", "Score source identity, passage entailment, units, dates, and appropriate abstention separately. An assistant should decline unsupported detail while retaining useful supported facts. Test the actual cited passage rather than counting references or accepting a plausible explanation as evidence."),
    ("5.47", "model_release", "Monitoring must wait for labels to mature before measuring outcomes, while detecting input faults immediately.", "A fictional model predicts five-day returns. On day two after release, prices arrive normally but volume becomes zero for many stocks. Specify immediate input checks and the later performance evaluation.", "Flag the volume anomaly now and follow the declared shadow or halt policy. Do not label incomplete five-day returns as final outcomes. Once horizons mature, compare performance and calibration by cohort, retaining the release version and any intervention dates."),
    ("5.48", "knight", "A complete workflow should survive an interaction between AI mistakes, duplicate messages, and stale data.", "Run a fictional acceptance scenario where an assistant proposes an unsupported trade, its request is retried, and the quote feed stalls. Define evidence required to show the account remains within its contract.", "Verify source validation, independent risk rejection, idempotent request handling, and stale-data gates through observable state and event logs. Continue reconciling accepted executions during the incident. Passing isolated component checks is useful, but this scenario tests how the controls interact."),
    ("5.29", "atomic_ledger", "A durable paper bot must reproduce its account after interruption without relying on in-memory state.", "For the project, restart a fictional rule bot after each of three boundaries: intent persistence, broker acceptance, and partial-fill persistence. Specify the recovered state and reconciliation action for each.", "Keep pending intent and execution identities durable, query uncertain broker outcomes, and replay committed events idempotently. Recovered cash and positions must agree with the authoritative journal. A successful clean shutdown does not demonstrate recovery from an interrupted transition."),
    ("5.30", "ml_pipeline", "An expanding-window project should generate a dated sequence of predictions that could have existed in operation.", "Create a fictional monthly schedule that retrains from all eligible prior data, predicts the next month, and records features, model version, predictions, and later labels.", "Fit every learned transformation within each expanding history and preserve the out-of-sample predictions before scoring. Include baseline and net economic evaluation using identical dates. Recomputing old predictions with the newest model would replace the operational history with hindsight."),
    ("5.31", "edgar", "An AI research packet is reviewable when each numeric statement resolves to dated source evidence.", "Assemble a fictional issuer packet containing a source manifest, three extracted facts, their passages and units, one unresolved question, and a deterministic reconciliation check.", "Preserve filing version and extraction configuration so another reviewer can repeat the process. Mark unsupported questions for abstention or further research. Keep generated interpretation separate from source facts, and do not let a polished narrative override a failed numerical or availability check."),
    ("5.32", "reporting", "A bot acceptance dossier combines economic results with evidence of controlled behavior during failure.", "Write a fictional final paper-release decision using one performance comparison, one accounting reconciliation, one injected incident, and a rollback drill. Link the corresponding artifacts and unmet conditions.", "A conditional acceptance should state the authorized environment, limits, monitoring, and next review date. Reject or defer when critical evidence is missing even if returns look attractive. The report describes tested behavior and remaining uncertainty, not a promise of live trading performance."),

    ("9.1", "c_safety", "A C port needs an explicit numerical and data contract before speed can be meaningfully compared.", "Choose a fictional Python moving-average backtest and write the C port's contract: accepted inputs, warm-up behavior, signal timing, monetary units, outputs, and comparison tolerances.", "Use a small fixture whose decisions and account transitions can be checked manually. Match semantics before profiling. A faster program computing a different window, rounding rule, or execution clock is a changed strategy rather than a demonstrated acceleration of the original."),
    ("9.2", "c_safety", "Compiler and linker settings are part of the reproducible artifact used to investigate a failure.", "Prepare a fictional C17 debug build with warnings, debug symbols, and AddressSanitizer. Record compiler version and the complete compile/link command beside a failing input fixture.", "Instrument at compilation and link the sanitizer runtime through the compiler driver. Preserve the diagnostic and a minimal reproducer. Use a separate documented release build for performance measurement; changing optimization or instrumentation can change both diagnostics and timing."),
    ("9.3", "atomic_ledger", "Integer monetary units avoid some rounding errors but still need overflow and range checks.", "A fictional order contains 25 shares at 12,345 cents plus a 99-cent fee. Calculate total cents and specify a multiplication/addition overflow check before updating a signed 64-bit balance.", "The total is 308,724 cents. Validate nonnegative quantities and price ranges, then check representability before arithmetic rather than after overflow. Exact integer storage does not choose a currency conversion or fee-rounding policy; those remain explicit parts of the account contract."),
    ("9.4", "c_safety", "Ownership rules prevent a cached feature from referring to a price buffer that no longer exists.", "A fictional parser allocates a price array, passes its pointer to a strategy, then frees it before evaluation. Identify the lifetime bug and compare caller-owned borrowing with transfer of ownership.", "Evaluation after the free is a use-after-free. Either keep the caller's buffer alive for the documented borrow duration or transfer responsibility with a clear release point. Test normal and error paths with sanitizers; avoid solving ambiguity through unexplained shared ownership."),
    ("9.5", "negative_prices", "Parsing a numeric field correctly is different from accepting it for the intended instrument.", "A fictional C reader receives NaN, an overflowing integer volume, a negative equity price, and a supported negative futures price. Specify syntax, range, finiteness, and product checks in order.", "Reject malformed or nonrepresentable values before conversion can corrupt state, then apply instrument-specific rules. A negative futures value is not automatically invalid merely because the equity contract rejects it. Return explicit errors with row identity and avoid partially accepted records."),
    ("9.6", "vectorization", "Memory layout changes how much irrelevant data a hot loop loads from the cache.", "A fictional record contains timestamp, price, volume, and a long symbol label, while the hot loop reads only prices. Compare array-of-structures with a separate contiguous price array.", "A price-only layout can reduce unnecessary memory traffic and support vectorization, but conversion and other access patterns also cost time. Benchmark the complete workload and retain a mapping to original records. Better locality is a hypothesis to measure, not a universal rule for every operation."),
    ("9.7", "vectorization", "Streaming calculations can remove repeated work while preserving the original window definition.", "For a fictional three-observation rolling sum over 10, 20, 30, 40, show the first complete sum and the update when 40 arrives. Specify missing-value and warm-up behavior.", "The first sum is 60; subtracting 10 and adding 40 gives 90. Keep the outgoing element accessible and test ring-buffer boundaries. Floating-point accumulation can drift over long runs, so compare with a reference and document any periodic recomputation or tolerance policy."),
    ("9.8", "knight", "A fast event loop must preserve the causal order that makes a backtest economically possible.", "A fictional signal uses today's closing price and requests a buy. Create a two-session fixture showing that the order can first fill at the next session's eligible event.", "The closing observation updates features and creates an intent after it is known. It cannot retroactively obtain the earlier open or an assumed pre-signal fill. Test event ordering explicitly, including missing next-session prices and pending orders across session boundaries."),
    ("9.9", "gpu", "Timing the intended workload requires a clock and measurement boundary that match the performance question.", "A fictional C benchmark includes file loading, feature computation, and reporting. Define separate component measurements and an end-to-end measurement using a monotonic clock, with a stated warm-up policy.", "Measure elapsed intervals with a monotonic source and prevent the optimizer from removing unused work. Report input size and which initialization steps are included. A kernel-only timing cannot answer whether the learner's complete research job finishes sooner."),
    ("9.10", "gpu", "A large local speedup can produce a modest total improvement when the optimized section is small.", "A fictional job spends 20% of its time in a kernel. The kernel becomes ten times faster. Use Amdahl's law to estimate total speedup and identify measurements needed beyond a single run.", "The estimated speedup is 1 / (0.8 + 0.2/10), about 1.22 times. Report repeated-run distributions, tails, workload, and hardware. Setup and transfer costs can reduce the observed gain further, while changing workload size can alter the original time fraction."),
    ("9.11", "c_safety", "Memory diagnostics and numerical fixtures catch different failure classes in a research engine.", "Create fictional fixtures for an empty input, a one-element window, and a malformed row, plus a known-return accounting example. State what sanitizers check and what the expected-output comparison checks.", "Sanitizers can detect certain memory misuse while the fixture checks business semantics and numerical results. Passing one does not imply passing the other. Exercise error cleanup and boundary lengths, and preserve regression inputs for any discovered crash or incorrect account transition."),
    ("9.12", "vectorization", "An optimization claim needs compiler evidence and an unchanged-result check alongside timing.", "A fictional price loop is slower than expected. Capture vectorization diagnostics, identify one dependency or layout obstacle, change one factor, and compare outputs and repeated timings.", "Record the exact flags, compiler, input, and benchmark scope. A diagnostic can explain why vectorization did or did not occur, but only measurements establish the observed effect. Reject an apparent gain that depends on invalid alias assumptions or altered numerical semantics."),
    ("9.13", "parallel", "Independent parameter trials can run concurrently when each owns its mutable state.", "A fictional C study evaluates 100 lookback values using four POSIX threads. Define each worker's immutable inputs, private buffers, trial IDs, and result ownership before implementing scheduling.", "Share read-only data and give each trial separate account and feature state. Collect results by trial ID rather than completion order, and propagate worker errors. The cited parallel concepts support the design; POSIX thread lifecycle and cleanup still follow the project's explicit implementation contract."),
    ("9.14", "parallel", "A shared progress counter or result slot can create a race even when the calculations themselves are independent.", "Two fictional workers both update a shared completed-trials counter and publish a result pointer. Specify synchronization that ensures readers see a fully initialized result.", "Use appropriate atomic or mutex-protected state and a defined publication relationship; volatile is not a synchronization mechanism. Assign one writer per result slot where possible. Test concurrent error paths and avoid letting a progress display compromise the correctness of the research output."),
    ("9.15", "parallel", "Randomness should follow experiment identity so scheduling changes do not silently change the study.", "A fictional parameter sweep runs with two and eight workers. Define per-trial seeds derived from a stable master seed and trial ID, plus a deterministic result collection order.", "Each trial receives the same random stream identity regardless of its worker or finish time. Sort outputs by stable ID and state how reductions are ordered. Parallel floating-point sums may still vary unless the reduction method explicitly controls order or tolerances."),
    ("9.16", "parallel", "Scheduling policy trades work balance against overhead when trial durations differ.", "A fictional OpenMP sweep mixes short and long backtests. Compare static and dynamic scheduling at one, two, and four threads, recording wall time and per-worker work rather than only CPU utilization.", "Dynamic scheduling may improve balance but adds coordination overhead and can change completion order. Keep trial inputs and identities fixed and compare end-to-end timings. Verify outputs before interpreting speedup, and report the hardware and workload that made the chosen schedule effective."),
    ("9.17", "vectorization", "SIMD transformations need an explicit tolerance for floating-point effects and valid memory assumptions.", "A fictional return-sum loop is vectorized with a different reduction order. Compare against a scalar reference using mixed large and small values, including cancellation, and justify the acceptance tolerance.", "Floating-point addition is not associative, so equivalent real-number formulas can produce different rounded results. Measure whether discrepancies affect decisions or account invariants. Do not use aggressive math flags merely to obtain a speedup without reviewing NaN, infinity, and rounding semantics."),
    ("9.18", "gpu", "Transfer overhead can overwhelm a faster device kernel for small research batches.", "A fictional CPU job takes 8 ms. GPU transfer and setup take 6 ms and its kernel takes 3 ms. Compare total latency, then describe a batching experiment with unchanged numerical checks.", "The GPU path takes 9 ms and is slower for this workload despite the 3 ms kernel. Batch enough independent work to test whether overhead can be amortized, while measuring full latency and throughput separately. Include result transfer and validation in the stated scope."),
    ("9.19", "parallel", "Distributed trial collection needs to distinguish a repeated delivery from a missing or conflicting result.", "A fictional manifest lists trials A, B, and C. Workers return A twice, B once, and no C. Define a collector that reports completeness and handles a conflicting duplicate A.", "Key results by manifest trial ID and input/version identity. Accept identical repeated deliveries idempotently, flag conflicting outputs, and leave C visibly incomplete. Never treat the number of received files as the number of successfully completed independent experiments."),
    ("9.20", "overload", "A bounded queue is both a memory contract and a policy for work that cannot be processed in time.", "A fictional producer writes quotes faster than a C consumer reads them. Define capacity, full-queue behavior, ownership of slots, and how publication makes an initialized entry visible.", "Choose blocking, rejection, or safe coalescing according to event semantics, and track queue age. Use synchronization with a valid happens-before relationship; a capacity check alone does not prevent races. Executions and accounting events need stronger retention than replaceable quote snapshots."),
    ("9.21", "atomic_ledger", "A pre-trade check and the resulting reservation must form one consistent account transition.", "Two fictional workers each see 1,000 available cents and simultaneously request an 800-cent reservation. Show the overspend under separate checks and define a serialized transition.", "Independent checks can both pass, reserving 1,600 against 1,000. Protect check-and-reserve as one operation using the chosen synchronization or transactional design. Exactly one request should fit unchanged, and failures must leave balances and reservation records consistent."),
    ("9.22", "idempotency", "A restarted engine must resolve an uncertain order using identity and evidence rather than guessing its outcome.", "A fictional C process crashes after a broker accepts an order but before the local acknowledgement is stored. Specify restart reconciliation and the effect of replaying an already processed fill.", "Retain the original intent ID, query or reconcile the broker's authoritative state, and retry only under the idempotency contract. A previously processed fill must not change cash or positions again. Record unresolved state explicitly instead of assuming rejection from a missing local acknowledgement."),
    ("9.23", "overload", "A low median loop time is insufficient when tail delays make market inputs stale.", "A fictional engine processes most ticks in 1 ms but pauses for 2 seconds during logging. Define a maximum input-age gate and a benchmark that captures the pause's operational effect.", "Measure latency distributions, queue age, dropped work, and stale-decision count under representative bursts. Keep diagnostic logging bounded and account for its cost. A system that eventually processes every event can still be unsuitable for decisions tied to a short freshness budget."),
    ("9.24", "reporting", "The C capstone should defend numerical correctness, reproducibility, and useful speed in one evidence package.", "Prepare a fictional handoff with scalar-reference parity, sanitizer fixtures, compiler manifest, repeated end-to-end benchmarks, parallel determinism checks, and one documented failure or limitation.", "Report the workload and hardware beside speed claims and show that optimized decisions preserve the intended strategy contract. Link every chart to raw measurements and build identity. The conclusion should say where the engine is useful and which untested workloads remain outside the evidence."),

    ("6.6", "bitcoin", "A payment commitment depends on exact bytes rather than how a human-readable value happens to look.", "In a local toy ledger, hash two encodings of the same fictional payment with different field order or whitespace. Define one canonical byte representation before signing or hashing.", "Different byte strings generally produce different digests even when a person reads the same meaning. Specify field order, lengths, encoding, and numeric units. A hash establishes a commitment to those bytes; it does not establish who authorized the payment or whether its contents are true."),
    ("6.7", "bitcoin", "An inclusion proof lets a verifier check one payment against a committed batch without downloading every payment.", "Build a local four-leaf Merkle tree for fictional transactions A, B, C, and D. List the sibling hashes and left/right directions needed to prove C's inclusion.", "For a binary tree pairing A/B and C/D, the proof needs D's hash and the hash of the A/B subtree with their directions. Recompute to the trusted root and reject a modified C. Membership alone does not establish transaction validity or trust in the root."),
    ("6.8", "bitcoin", "Signature verification connects a transaction's bytes to a key, while ledger rules determine whether it may spend.", "Using dummy local keys, sign a fictional payment of 10 units and verify it. Then change the amount to 11 and explain the expected result and the additional balance check.", "Verification should fail for the altered signed bytes. A valid original signature still needs ownership, nonce or unspent-output, balance, and replay checks under the ledger design. Keep test keys local and distinguish a cryptographic authorization check from complete transaction validation."),
    ("6.9", "replay", "A payment signature should bind the network and sequence in which it is authorized.", "Create fictional test chains 101 and 202 with the same dummy public key. Sign a transaction for chain 101, then test it on chain 202 and repeat it on chain 101.", "The cross-chain attempt should fail the domain check, while the repeated same-chain transaction should fail the nonce or spent-input rule. Chain identity and transaction sequence prevent different replay classes. The cited EIP illustrates one legacy Ethereum mechanism, not every possible signing format."),
    ("6.10", "erc20", "A token balance belongs to a contract's accounting system and differs from the native coin used for network fees.", "In a fictional local chain, a learner owns 100 classroom tokens but no native coin. Explain why a token transfer can still be impossible and identify the separate ledgers involved.", "Token ownership is recorded by the token contract, while transaction execution normally requires the chain's native fee asset. A local faucet can supply test gas under the course setup. More tokens do not automatically fund execution or confer validator authority."),
    ("6.11", "bitcoin", "Consensus resolves competing histories, so an observed payment may need more evidence before a merchant treats it as settled.", "In a local toy network, two miners produce competing blocks at the same height. Describe how the chosen chain rule affects a payment included on only one branch.", "The payment may be absent from the eventually selected history and return to pending status if still valid. Track cumulative work under the toy protocol and state the confirmation policy. A small demonstration network does not reproduce the security or economic assumptions of a large operational chain."),
    ("6.12", "atomic_ledger", "A block's state changes must be validated and committed together to avoid a partially accepted history.", "A fictional block contains one valid payment followed by an overspend. Specify whether the toy chain rejects the whole block and how candidate state is isolated from committed balances.", "Under the stated all-or-nothing block contract, validate against temporary state and commit only if every rule passes. Rejection leaves balances, nonces, and chain tip unchanged. Include duplicate-spend and malformed-transaction fixtures to verify that error paths do not leak partial updates."),
    ("6.13", "bitcoin", "A signed-coin project needs a reproducible demonstration of both a valid payment and a rejected attack.", "Build a local-only evidence script that creates dummy keys, initializes balances, signs a payment, mines or validates a block, and then attempts a tampered payment and a duplicate spend.", "Save the initial state, transaction bytes, verification results, and final balances. The valid payment should change state once; invalid attempts should leave it unchanged. Explain the toy chain's consensus and storage limits instead of presenting the classroom artifact as a deployable currency network."),
    ("6.14", "erc20", "Token display amounts and on-chain integer units must agree before transfers and allowances are constructed.", "A fictional token uses six decimals. Convert 1.25 displayed tokens into base units and explain why floating-point multiplication is a poor input parser for arbitrary decimal strings.", "The transfer amount is 1,250,000 base units. Parse decimal input with an explicit precision and range policy so rounding cannot silently alter authority. Decimals affect display conventions; they do not change the integer arithmetic used by the contract's balances."),
    ("6.15", "erc20", "Fixed supply is a property of the implemented authority paths, not simply a label in the project description.", "Design fictional classroom credits with 1,000 tokens minted at local deployment and no later mint function. List balance, total-supply, zero-address, transfer, and allowance behaviors to inspect.", "Confirm the deployed implementation and any upgrade authority cannot introduce an unreviewed mint path. Check supply invariants under transfers and the declared burn policy. Conforming to an interface does not itself establish fixed supply, fair distribution, or economic value."),
    ("6.16", "erc20", "Approving a spender changes permission, while the later delegated transfer changes ownership balances.", "On a local chain, Alice has 100 fictional tokens and approves Bob for 30. Bob transfers 20 from Alice to Carol. Calculate balances and remaining allowance under a standard decrementing allowance policy.", "Alice has 80, Carol has 20, and Bob's remaining allowance is 10; approval alone did not transfer tokens to Bob. Verify emitted events and contract reads. Use dummy accounts and explicitly test an attempted transfer above the remaining allowance."),
    ("6.17", "erc20", "Administrative permissions can change the economic promises attached to a token after its first deployment.", "A fictional token advertises fixed supply but has an upgrade administrator and a treasury mint role. Draw the authority graph and identify which claims need correction or stronger controls.", "The current interface is insufficient to support an unconditional fixed-supply claim if authorized paths can expand supply or replace logic. Document each role, transfer mechanism, delay, and revocation rule. Distinguish current balances from future powers that can alter holders' exposure."),
    ("6.1", "erc20", "Circulating supply and total authorized supply answer different questions about a holder's future share.", "A fictional project has 1,000 circulating tokens and schedules 500 more for release from a reserve. A learner holds 100. Compare their fraction of circulating tokens before and after, assuming no other changes.", "The fraction falls from 10% to about 6.67%. This arithmetic does not predict price because demand, liquidity, and expectations also matter. Clarify whether the 500 tokens already exist in total supply or are newly minted; unlocking and minting are different events."),
    ("6.18", "erc20", "A vesting schedule becomes operationally meaningful when dates, units, and treasury balances reconcile.", "A fictional team allocation contains 1,200 tokens, with a three-month cliff and 100 released monthly for twelve releases starting at the cliff. Calculate released and locked amounts after the fourth release.", "Four releases total 400, leaving 800 locked under the stated convention. Specify the first release timestamp and whether release is automatic or claim-based. Reconcile contract balances and treasury records rather than inferring circulation from an ambiguous calendar description."),
    ("6.2", "uniswap", "A constant-product quote worsens as the trade consumes more of one reserve.", "A fictional fee-free v2-style pool holds 100 units of A and 10,000 of B. Add 10 A and compute B received using the constant-product invariant, ignoring rounding.", "The invariant is 1,000,000. New B reserves are about 9,090.91, so output is 909.09 B, an average of 90.91 B per A versus the initial spot ratio of 100. Real fees and integer rounding further affect executable output."),
    ("6.3", "uniswap", "An apparent price difference is only an arbitrage opportunity if both legs and all costs can be executed.", "A fictional token costs 100 on one venue and sells for 103 on another. Total estimated fees, price impact, and transfer costs are 4 per token. Evaluate the simple margin and list remaining timing risks.", "The estimated net margin is −1 per token before uncertainty, so the quoted difference alone is insufficient. Nonatomic legs add inventory and price risk, while transfer delays or failed transactions can prevent completion. Record executable size rather than comparing only displayed spot prices."),
    ("6.4", "uniswap", "A swap's signed limits determine what can execute after prices move while the transaction waits.", "A fictional local swap is expected to return 100 tokens with minimum output 98. Another trade moves the pool so only 97 would be returned. State the expected outcome under a correctly enforced minimum.", "The swap should revert under that output condition rather than deliver 97, though transaction fees may still be incurred. A gas limit bounds execution resources and is distinct from a slippage limit. Test ordering changes and deadlines in the local simulation."),
    ("6.19", "oracle", "External prices need freshness, units, and authority checks before they can affect collateral accounting.", "A fictional lending contract receives price 2,000 with eight decimals and a timestamp two hours old, while its declared maximum age is 15 minutes. Define the validation result and fallback behavior.", "Reject the value as stale under this contract before using it for new risk decisions. Normalize units and verify asset/feed identity as separate checks. Any pause or fallback policy must be explicit and tested; silently using the last number can conceal a broken data dependency."),
    ("6.5", "oracle", "A collateral ratio can cross a liquidation threshold even when the borrower has made no new transaction.", "A fictional loan owes 600 against collateral initially worth 1,000, with a minimum collateral-to-debt ratio of 150%. Compute the initial ratio and the ratio after a 20% collateral-price fall.", "The ratio falls from about 166.67% to 133.33%, crossing the assumed threshold. Liquidation fees, oracle delay, slippage, and debt interest can worsen the outcome. These are teaching parameters rather than the terms of a named lending protocol."),
    ("6.20", "erc20", "Invariant tests examine what must remain true across many transaction sequences and rejected actions.", "For fictional fixed-supply classroom credits, generate local transfer and approval sequences including insufficient balances, unauthorized spending, and repeated calls. State supply and balance invariants after each step.", "Transfers preserve total supply and redistribute balances under the declared implementation; rejected actions should leave state unchanged. Track allowance effects separately. Preserve failing seeds and compiler/dependency versions so a reviewer can reproduce an invariant failure and its fix."),
    ("6.21", "bitcoin", "Monetary policy and recovery rules determine which history and balances a restarted toy chain accepts.", "A fictional local chain pays a fixed block reward and restarts from disk. Specify how it validates cumulative issuance, selects a valid tip, and handles a corrupted last block without silently creating coins.", "Revalidate the persisted chain or trusted checkpoint under explicit rules, then rebuild derived balances and issuance totals. Reject invalid rewards and preserve the corrupt artifact for diagnosis. A convenience reset changes the ledger's history and must not be described as transparent consensus recovery."),
    ("6.22", "erc20", "A token release artifact should let a reviewer connect source code, deployed bytecode, and administrative powers.", "Prepare a fictional local release bundle with source hash, compiler settings, dependency lockfile, deployment arguments, chain ID, address, initial distribution, and role inventory.", "Rebuild and compare the intended artifact, then query local supply, balances, and roles against the manifest. Record any upgrade or mint powers and who controls them. An address alone is insufficient evidence that the reviewed source or claimed authority model was deployed."),
    ("6.23", "erc20", "Publication material should explain what the artifact does and who can change it without implying economic guarantees.", "Draft a fictional classroom-token README covering local setup, supply mechanics, permissions, known limitations, and the difference between a technical demonstration and an offered investment.", "Use verifiable statements tied to the build and authority manifest. Explain upgrade, pause, and treasury controls in language a learner can inspect. Avoid price or return promises unsupported by the artifact, and leave any real distribution or legal requirements outside an unreviewed classroom release."),
    ("6.24", "reporting", "The final Web3 dossier should defend cryptographic checks, ledger behavior, and disclosed trust assumptions together.", "Assemble a fictional capstone packet with one valid payment, one replay rejection, one failed block, token supply/allowance tests, AMM arithmetic, oracle staleness handling, and reproducible local commands.", "Connect each result to input fixtures and build identity, and clearly separate native-coin rules from token-contract rules. Explain where trusted operators, price feeds, and administrative keys remain. The dossier demonstrates tested local behavior and limitations rather than certifying a production network."),
]
